{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`autodeploy.sh` keeps ONE piece of global state for TWO independently-deploying components (the\nreview-bot and the mcp blue-green target). Split out of `cf34-9b8b-1aa7-4d95` with the\noperator's approval: that ticket scopes the review-bot DEFERRAL so control reaches the mcp\nblock; this one makes the state each component depends on per-component.\n\nFour rounds of code review on the combined change each found the same defect shape, each fix\nexposing the next. They are one concern and belong in one ticket.\n\n## The three couplings\n\n**Completion.** `deployed-sha` is written once, at the footer, so ANY early exit loses the\ncompletion of whichever component DID deploy, and the next tick redeploys it. A review-bot\nredeploy stop-and-drains the container, killing an in-flight review (bug 34cd) — the exact\nharm the drain gate exists to prevent, arriving from the other direction.\n\n**Failure.** `record_backoff_failure` writes ONE `deploy-backoff` keyed on the global `$TARGET`,\nand the top-level gate exits the whole script on it. On `main` it is called from BOTH blocks\n(~5 sites in the bot block, ~10 in the mcp block), so an mcp failure throttles the BOT and a\nbot failure suppresses mcp.\n\n**Incompleteness.** A component skipped by an open backoff window has not deployed, but nothing\nrecords that, so the footer stamps `$TARGET` into its marker for a deploy that never ran. The\nnext tick then computes no delta and drops the pending change PERMANENTLY.\n\n## Shape of the fix\n\n- `mcp-deployed-sha` and `bot-deployed-sha`, each written the moment that component completes,\n  with each component's delta gate RE-POINTED to diff from its own marker rather than the\n  global `$DEPLOYED` baseline. An absent or unparseable marker falls back to `$DEPLOYED`.\n  The global `deployed-sha` KEEPS its existing role unchanged: it is the TICK-level record,\n  advanced only at the footer and only on a tick that completed, and it still drives the\n  top-level \"up to date; no-op\" early exit (`if [ \"$TARGET\" = \"$DEPLOYED\" ]`). It is not\n  removed and not split — the component markers are added ALONGSIDE it. On a complete tick the\n  footer stamps all three in lockstep, so a component marker can never be left behind the\n  global one; on an incomplete tick the guard exits before the footer and none of the three\n  advances. `$DEPLOYED` also remains the fallback baseline for a component whose marker is\n  absent or unparseable, which is what makes the first tick after upgrade behave as today.\n\n- A separate `mcp-deploy-backoff` recorder; the existing file then records bot failures only,\n  which is what makes it safe to move its gate off the top of the script. Each gate sits with\n  the component it throttles and skips that component instead of exiting the tick.\n- `bot_incomplete` / `mcp_incomplete`, honoured by the footer guard, so a backoff-skip with a\n  delta still pending never records completion. Each gate tests the DELTA before the window, so\n  \"nothing to do\" stays distinguishable from \"skipped with work pending\".\n- Both recorders keep emitting the `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR` marker. That token — not the reason\n  string — is what the alarm counts: `infra/scripts/observability.sh:230` does a bare\n  `grep -cE '^AUTODEPLOY_ERROR \\{'` over the unit journal and publishes the count as\n  `rebar/host:deploy_errors` (`:240`, §4d). It never parses the reason, so alarm continuity is\n  preserved by continuing to emit the marker at all. The reason stays `deploy_failed` and\n  gains a `component=mcp` marker for OPERATOR legibility — telling the two paths apart when\n  reading the journal — not for the metric.\n\n## Consumers outside autodeploy.sh\n\nThe marker's only non-test consumer is `infra/scripts/observability.sh` (§4d, lines 230/240),\nwhich counts `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR` occurrences into `rebar/host:deploy_errors`. It is deliberately\nkept distinct from the deferral and retire-cap tokens (`:259-260`, `:282`, `:338`) so routine\ndeferrals and retire-caps never inflate that alarm — this change adds no new token, so that\nseparation is unchanged. Test consumers of the marker: `test_autodeploy_review_drain.py`,\n`test_autodeploy_health_gate.py`, `test_observability_rotation_2dc7.py`,\n`test_observability_marker_anchor_8c2f.py`.\n\nThe one test consumer of the shared STATE FILE is `tests/unit/test_autodeploy_prune.py:86`,\nwhich reads `state/deploy-backoff` after a REVIEW-BOT failure. That file keeps its name and\nkeeps recording bot failures, so the test needs no change and continues to pass unmodified —\nverified, 4 passed. Only the mcp block's failures move to the new file, and no existing test\nreads that path. Had the split renamed `deploy-backoff` instead of narrowing what writes to it,\nthis consumer would have broken silently; narrowing rather than renaming is what avoids that.\n\n## Deliberately out of scope\n\nA bot FATAL path still exits the tick, so mcp misses that single tick, after which the bot is in\nbackoff and skipped and mcp proceeds. Bounding it to one tick is enough; making the bot's fatal\npaths return rather than exit is a larger restructure of a live deploy script.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An mcp failure does not write the SHARED backoff.\n- [ ] An mcp failure does not cause the review-bot to redeploy on later ticks.\n- [ ] A component skipped by its own backoff window is not recorded as deployed (both halves).\n- [ ] An open backoff window with NO pending delta is not reported as a skip.\n- [ ] An mcp backoff throttles mcp while the review-bot still deploys on the same tick.\n- [ ] mcp failures still emit an `AUTODEPLOY_ERROR` marker, so the `deploy_errors` metric\n      (`observability.sh:230,240`) counts them exactly as before, and the reason line names\n      `component=mcp` so an operator can tell which path failed.\n\n## Verification\n\n    .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/scripts/ -q -p no:randomly\n\nEach new test is mutation-checked: removing the behaviour it guards returns it to RED."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787808046784750001,"uuid":"efd394e8-a458-45fc-b4d3-88b418792872"}